# CAIOS environment. Copy to configs/env/caios.env and fill in.
#
#   cp configs/env/caios.env.template configs/env/caios.env
#
# caios.env is gitignored. Never commit real values.
# Every public hostname in this repository derives from CAIOS_PUBLIC_DOMAIN
# below, so moving to a real domain is one line.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Addresses
#
# Three addresses and one domain. The addresses are where things run; the
# domain is what the outside world types. They are not the same thing and
# conflating them is what this section exists to prevent.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Private IP of caios_server. Runs the Compose control plane behind Caddy.
# Reached directly only from inside the subnet.
CAIOS_CTRL_IP=

# Private IP of caios_edge. Traefik. Every deployment is routed from here.
CAIOS_EDGE_IP=

# Public IP of the nginx proxy VM (the jumpserver). This is the only address
# a visitor ever connects to, for BOTH tiers — it terminates TLS and routes on
# the Host header to Caddy or to Traefik. It is not configured from this
# repository; see docs/nginx-proxy.md and ansible/playbook-jumpserver.yml.
CAIOS_PUBLIC_IP=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# C0. The one place the platform's public domain is written down.
#
# sslip.io resolves anything ending in an IP back to that IP, so the default
# needs no DNS account and no zone access. That is what let the platform run
# for six weeks without a domain, and it is what the rollback returns to.
#
# A real domain is a one-line change:
#
#   CAIOS_PUBLIC_DOMAIN=caios.ca
#
# then render-configs.sh, reissue the two internal certificates, re-run
# keycloak-bootstrap.sh, and restart. See docs/certificate-plan.md.
#
# Nothing else in the repository spells a public hostname out.
# tests/test_public_domain.py fails if anything starts doing so again.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CAIOS_PUBLIC_DOMAIN=${CAIOS_PUBLIC_IP}.sslip.io

# Derived. Do not edit; they follow from CAIOS_PUBLIC_DOMAIN.
CAIOS_DASHBOARD_HOST=dashboard.${CAIOS_PUBLIC_DOMAIN}
CAIOS_API_HOST=api.${CAIOS_PUBLIC_DOMAIN}
CAIOS_AUTH_HOST=auth.${CAIOS_PUBLIC_DOMAIN}
CAIOS_VAULT_HOST=vault.${CAIOS_PUBLIC_DOMAIN}

# PAPI's lb.domain. A deployment resolves as
#   <service>-<uuid>.<meta.domain>-<this value>
# and meta.domain is `pacs` (ansible/inventory/hosts.ini), so the wildcard
# certificate has to cover *.pacs-${CAIOS_DEPLOYMENTS_DOMAIN}.
CAIOS_DEPLOYMENTS_DOMAIN=deployments.${CAIOS_PUBLIC_DOMAIN}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Let's Encrypt, for a real domain (docs/certificate-plan.md, stages C1-C3)
#
# Unused while CAIOS_PUBLIC_DOMAIN is an sslip.io name: Let's Encrypt issues
# wildcards only over DNS-01, which needs control of the zone, and nobody
# controls sslip.io.
#
# The certificate lives on the nginx proxy VM, not on Caddy and not on
# Traefik — those two keep their CAIOS CA certificates for the internal leg,
# which is what nginx's `proxy_ssl_verify on` validates against.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Expiry notices go here. Any address that is read.
CAIOS_ACME_EMAIL=

# Cloudflare API token, scoped to Edit zone DNS on that one zone and nothing
# else. Written to /etc/letsencrypt/cloudflare.ini mode 0600 on the proxy VM.
# A real secret: it can rewrite every record in the zone.
CAIOS_CF_API_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scheme  (T5)
#
# The one switch. Everything user-facing derives from it: Caddy's site blocks,
# Keycloak's issuer, PAPI's advertised endpoints, the dashboard's apiURL, the
# federated-learning bundles and every check script.
#
#   https   the platform serves TLS from our own CA. Every visitor must first
#           install caios-ca.pem, or the dashboard's background calls to the
#           API host fail with no prompt and no explanation.
#   http    no certificate to install. This is what the demo runs on.
#
# Flip it, re-render, restart. That is the whole rollback:
#
#   sed -i 's/^CAIOS_SCHEME=.*/CAIOS_SCHEME=https/' configs/env/caios.env
#   bash scripts/render-configs.sh
#   cd compose && sudo docker compose --env-file ../configs/env/caios.env up -d
#
# The certificates stay on disk and Caddy keeps serving BOTH schemes either
# way — the inactive one answers with a 302 to the active one. That is not
# tidiness: Chrome upgrades http:// navigations to https:// on its own, and
# without the bounce a visitor lands on a working TLS page whose API calls are
# then blocked as mixed content. A broken dashboard that looks like our fault.
#
# One deliberate exception, and it is in the job templates rather than here:
# the federated-learning server keeps TLS. It speaks gRPC on :443 and its
# clients live inside workspaces that already carry the CA, so it is never a
# barrier to anybody — and h2c from client to Traefik is not worth betting the
# headline demo on. See docs/decisions.md D-67.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CAIOS_SCHEME=https

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Keycloak
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN=admin
# Generate: openssl rand -base64 24
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
KEYCLOAK_REALM=caios
# Postgres behind Keycloak. Generate: openssl rand -base64 24
KEYCLOAK_DB_PASSWORD=
# Client secret for the `papi` confidential client. Must match the value in
# configs/keycloak/caios-realm.json after import.
KEYCLOAK_PAPI_SECRET=

# T6. The approval service's Keycloak service account. It is the only thing on
# the platform holding realm-management rights, so treat it as a real secret.
# scripts/keycloak-bootstrap.sh sets it on the `caios-registration` client.
# Generate: openssl rand -base64 24
KEYCLOAK_REGISTRATION_SECRET=

# Demo user passwords, consumed by scripts/keycloak-bootstrap.sh.
# Named CAIOS_PW_<USERNAME uppercased, hyphens as underscores>. Left empty, the
# script generates one and prints it once.
CAIOS_PW_RESEARCHER=
CAIOS_PW_SITE_A=
CAIOS_PW_SITE_B=
CAIOS_PW_SITE_C=
# The platform administrator. Holds `access:vo.caios.ca:ap-d`, which is what the
# approval service requires — and which outranks ap-u, so this account can
# deploy as well as approve.
CAIOS_PW_PLATFORM_ADMIN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vault
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Written by scripts/vault-bootstrap.sh on first run. Keep it somewhere safe:
# losing it means losing every deployment secret.
VAULT_ROOT_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PAPI
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PAPI runs on caios_server, the same node as the Nomad server, so it reaches
# Nomad over loopback and reuses the certificates Ansible already wrote there.
# This is why these paths are local and not copied from anywhere.
NOMAD_ADDR=https://127.0.0.1:4646
NOMAD_CACERT=/etc/nomad.d/certs/nomad-ca.pem
NOMAD_CLIENT_CERT=/etc/nomad.d/certs/cli.pem
NOMAD_CLIENT_KEY=/etc/nomad.d/certs/cli-key.pem

# Consumed by main.yaml's CORS list.
DASHBOARD_URL=${CAIOS_SCHEME}://${CAIOS_DASHBOARD_HOST}

# LEAVE IS_PROD UNSET.
# PAPI defaults it to false. In that mode, missing tokens for services we do
# not run (Harbor, Jenkins, provenance, LiteLLM) are warnings. Set it to true
# and PAPI refuses to start, with an error that does not point at the cause.
# IS_PROD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not used in MVP. Left here so nobody wonders whether they were forgotten.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ZENODO_TOKEN=            dataset publishing
# GITHUB_TOKEN=            catalogue metadata rate limits
# HARBOR_ROBOT_PASSWORD=   container registry (not running)
# JENKINS_TOKEN=           CI/CD publishing (not running)
# PROVENANCE_TOKEN=        provenance API (not running)
# MAILING_TOKEN=           deployment notification emails
# WATTNET_PASSWORD=        carbon footprint data (external API)
# ACCOUNTING_PTH=          historical usage stats (V1, if requested)
# LLM_API_KEY=             LLM chat (V1 stretch)

# Password for the JupyterLab/VS Code workspaces the federated demo deploys
# (scripts/deploy-fl-demo.sh). PAPI requires at least 9 characters.
CAIOS_FL_IDE_PASSWORD=

# GitHub repo the module marketplace is read from (patches/ai4-papi/0007).
# Leave as upstream for the stock 46-module catalogue, or point at a curated
# fork. See catalog/keep.txt and scripts/curate-catalogue.sh.
MODULES_CATALOGUE_REPO=ai4os-hub/modules-catalog

# Where PAPI reads the marketplace from (patches/ai4-papi/0014). Points at the
# mirror Caddy serves at /mirror/ on the dashboard host, built by
# scripts/mirror-catalogue.sh.
#
# Blank or unset means raw.githubusercontent.com, which is upstream's behaviour
# and which went unreachable from every node for three hours on 2026-09-01 —
# taking Modules, Tools and both use cases with it.
CATALOGUE_BASE_URL=${CAIOS_SCHEME}://${CAIOS_DASHBOARD_HOST}/mirror

# Bioimage.io models the AI4Life loader offers, in dropdown order
# (patches/ai4-papi/0008). Leave empty for upstream behaviour (all 68).
# Generate with scripts/render-ai4life-models.sh from catalog/ai4life-models.txt.
AI4LIFE_MODELS=
